CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a simple three-dimensional drawing of two conducting spheres joined by a wire. A large shaded sphere occupies the upper right of the frame, and a much smaller sphere in the same style sits at the lower left, roughly a third of the diameter of the first. Both are drawn with a soft interior highlight and are ringed by plus signs spaced around their surfaces to indicate positive charge. A thin grey curve sags gently between them, running from the left side of the large sphere down to the right side of the small one and standing for the connecting wire. Each sphere carries a black dot at its center with a short arrow drawn from that dot out to the surface: the arrow in the large sphere is labeled 'R1' and points up and to the right, while the one in the small sphere is labeled 'R2' and points down and to the left. Giving the two spheres markedly different radii while charging both positively is what sets up the comparison. The arrangement, held at a common potential by the wire, is used to relate the surface charge density on each sphere to its radius.

PROJECTION TYPE: perspective
Use the scaffold's true perspective projection after building all geometry. Use this only when the source has real vanishing-point convergence or distance-based size shrinkage; then depth should visibly affect apparent size.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Large conducting sphere (radius R1) in the upper right, shaded lavender-blue with a soft off-centre interior highlight
  - Small conducting sphere (radius R2) in the lower left, same shading style but paler/greyer, roughly one third the diameter of the large sphere
  - Ring of '+' signs distributed around the visible surface of the large sphere (evenly spaced, sitting just outside the silhouette)
  - Ring of '+' signs distributed around the visible surface of the small sphere
  - Black dot marking the centre of each sphere
  - Radius arrow inside the large sphere: from its centre dot up-and-to-the-right to the surface, labelled 'R₁' in italic serif
  - Radius arrow inside the small sphere: from its centre dot down-and-to-the-left to the surface, labelled 'R₂' in italic serif
  - Thin grey connecting wire: a gently sagging curve from the left side of the large sphere down to the right side of the small sphere
  - Potential readout labels near each sphere (V₁, V₂) showing the common or separate potentials
  - Surface charge density readout (σ) for the selected sphere
  - Highlight ring / glow applied to whichever sphere is clicked

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: large sphere centre at (+2.2, +0.5, 0) with base radius R₁ = 1.0 unit; small sphere centre at (-2.6, -0.9, 0) with R₂ = R₁ / ratio (default ratio 3 → R₂ ≈ 0.33). Slider changes R₂ (keep R₁ fixed at 1.0) so the frame stays stable; scene spans roughly x ∈ [-3.3, 3.4], y ∈ [-1.6, 1.7]. Wire: a sagging quadratic/catenary curve in the z=0 plane from the left edge of the large sphere (≈(1.25, 0.35, 0)) to the upper-right edge of the small sphere (≈(-2.35, -0.72, 0)), dipping ~0.25 units below the straight chord; tube radius ~0.015, colour #8a8a8a. Sphere material: radial gradient highlight up-and-left of centre, large sphere #f2f4fb core → #9aa6d4 rim (lavender-blue), small sphere #fafafa core → #bfbfc6 rim (neutral grey); both slightly translucent/matte, no specular hotspot. '+' signs: flat black glyphs (stroke ~0.03 units, arm length ~0.09 for the large sphere, scaled to ~0.07 on the small one), placed just outside the silhouette and around the visible surface at radius 1.02R, always billboarded to face the camera. Number of plus signs ∝ total charge Q ∝ R: 14 on the large sphere at R₁ = 1.0, and round(14 · R₂/R₁) with a minimum of 4 on the small sphere — so at ratio 3 the small sphere shows ~5, spread over a circumference 3× smaller, i.e. 3× the linear crowding. Physics: connected ⇒ V₁ = V₂ = kQ_total/(R₁+R₂) with Q_i ∝ R_i; σ_i = Q_i/(4πR_i²) ∝ 1/R_i, so σ_small/σ_large = R₁/R₂ = slider value. Display σ in arbitrary units normalized to σ_large = 1. Radius arrows: black, head length ~0.08; large sphere's arrow points up-right at ≈ +35° from horizontal with label 'R₁' set just below-right of the shaft midpoint; small sphere's arrow points down-left at ≈ -125° (i.e. down and to the left) with label 'R₂' just above-left of its shaft. Centre dots: black spheres of radius 0.035 (large) and 0.02 (small). Potential labels V₁, V₂ in small dark-grey italic text above each sphere.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "radiusRatio",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Radius ratio R₁ / R₂",
    "range": [
      1,
      6,
      0.1
    ],
    "default": 3,
    "teaches": "At a common potential the charge divides as Q ∝ R, so the smaller sphere ends up with fewer total charges but a much denser crowding of '+' signs on its surface — surface charge density goes as 1/R."
  },
  {
    "id": "wireConnected",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Connecting wire",
    "options": [
      "Connected (common potential)",
      "Separated"
    ],
    "default": "Connected (common potential)",
    "teaches": "Only the conducting wire forces V₁ = V₂ and lets charge flow until the potentials match; disconnected, each sphere keeps whatever charge it has and the two potentials differ."
  },
  {
    "id": "selectSphere",
    "type": "click",
    "teaches": "Clicking a sphere highlights its radius arrow and shows its Q, V and σ, making the R-versus-σ comparison between the two conductors explicit."
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The charges live on the full 3D surface of each conductor, not on a flat ring — rotating shows the '+' signs wrapping around both spheres."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Equal spheres, connected",
    "narration": "With R₁ = R₂ the wire equalizes the potentials and each sphere carries the same charge and the same surface charge density — the '+' signs are equally spaced on both.",
    "state": {
      "radiusRatio": 1,
      "wireConnected": "Connected (common potential)"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "The figure as drawn: R₁ = 3R₂",
    "narration": "Grow the large sphere to three times the small one. The wire keeps V₁ = V₂, so Q ∝ R: the big sphere holds three times the charge, yet its '+' signs are spread thinly while the small sphere's are crowded three times as densely.",
    "state": {
      "radiusRatio": 3,
      "wireConnected": "Connected (common potential)"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Push the ratio further",
    "narration": "At R₁ = 6R₂ the contrast is extreme — the small sphere's surface charge density (and therefore its surface field) is six times that of the large sphere, which is why sharp, small-radius points discharge first.",
    "state": {
      "radiusRatio": 6,
      "wireConnected": "Connected (common potential)"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Cut the wire",
    "narration": "Remove the wire and the spheres are isolated: charge can no longer flow, the potentials are free to differ, and the distribution frozen on each conductor no longer obeys V₁ = V₂.",
    "state": {
      "radiusRatio": 6,
      "wireConnected": "Separated"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "azimuthDeg": 90,
  "elevationDeg": 8,
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.6
}

NOTES: Render spheres as THREE.Mesh with a custom fake-gradient shader or MeshBasicMaterial + a radial-gradient canvas texture mapped so the highlight sits up-left — matching the flat textbook airbrush look rather than physical lighting. Plus signs and R-labels as CanvasTexture sprites (always camera-facing) so they stay crisp while orbiting; regenerate/reposition them whenever radiusRatio changes. Wire as a TubeGeometry along a CatmullRom/QuadraticBezier curve; when the toggle is set to 'Separated', animate the wire fading out and splitting at midpoint, and fade the shared-V label into two independent V₁ ≠ V₂ readouts. Redistribution on ratio change should tween plus-sign positions (lerp over ~300 ms) so the learner sees charge flow rather than a jump. Click picking via Raycaster on the two sphere meshes; selected sphere gets an emissive rim (thin outline mesh at 1.03R) and its radius arrow thickens, while the other dims to ~50% opacity. Keep background white and use orthographic-feeling low FOV (~28°) so the two spheres read at true relative size.

Follow the interaction plan above.

